@page "/blocks/block-editor"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Blocks

<DocsHeader Title="Block editor" Eyebrow="Website">
    <Lead>A form for one block of a page, generated from its type: its settings, and items to add, move and remove.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    <code>StilBlockEditor</code> edits a block of <a href="blocks/pages-as-data">a page as data</a>. It reads what
    the block's type has from its definition in the <code>StilBlockCatalog</code>, so the built-in types and your
    own get a form without one being written. It changes the block in place and raises <code>BlockChanged</code>
    after each change; with <code>@@bind-Block</code>, a <code>StilBlockView</code> beside it follows as you type.
    It needs an interactive render mode, and its styles are in the blocks stylesheet
    (<code>&lt;StilHead Blocks /&gt;</code>).
</p>

<h2 id="example">Edit a block</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(BlockEditing)">
    <p>
        Pick a type, change the form, and watch the preview and the JSON. This is the ground a site builder stands
        on: a list of the page's blocks, this form for the one you pick, and the page saved with
        <code>StilPageJson</code>.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="fields">The fields</h2>
<p>Each kind of setting (<code>StilBlockSettingKind</code>) has its own control, with the setting's label and help:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Text</strong>, <strong>Url</strong>: a text field, set as you type.</li>
    <li><strong>LongText</strong>: a text area; blank lines separate paragraphs.</li>
    <li><strong>Boolean</strong>: a switch.</li>
    <li><strong>Choice</strong>: a select of the choices; the first is the default and is not stored.</li>
    <li><strong>Icon</strong>: a select of the built-in icons, with the chosen one beside it.</li>
    <li><strong>Image</strong>: the picture's address and its alt text, kept together.</li>
    <li><strong>List</strong>: a text area with one item per line, saved when you leave it.</li>
    <li><strong>Number</strong>, <strong>Date</strong>: a number field and a date picker.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    An empty setting is removed from the block, so the JSON holds only what is set. The items of a block (the
    features of a feature grid, the buttons of a hero) each have their own group, with buttons to move them up or
    down and to remove them, and an add button under them until the type's <code>Max</code> is reached.
</p>

<h2 id="validation">Required settings</h2>
<p>
    A required setting has a <code>required</code> field. Once it has been emptied, it is marked invalid with a
    message; <code>ShowValidation</code> marks every empty one, such as when the user saves.
    <code>StilBlockDefinition.IsComplete(block)</code> tells whether a block and its items have all they need.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="razor" Code="@Save" />

<h2 id="accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
<p>
    Every field has a label, its help and its error message are linked with <code>aria-describedby</code>, and each
    item is a group named by its legend, such as "Button 2". The focus follows an item that moves, to the other
    button when it reaches the top or the bottom, goes to the new item when one is added, and to the next item's
    remove button when one is removed. A status region announces each of these. The button texts are translated.
</p>

<h2 id="api">API</h2>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilBlockEditor)" />

@code {
    private const string Save = """
        <StilBlockEditor @bind-Block="block" ShowValidation="showValidation" />
        <StilButton OnClick="Save">Save</StilButton>

        @code {
            private StilBlockData block = StilBlockData.Create("hero");
            private bool showValidation;

            private void Save()
            {
                showValidation = true;
                if (StilBlockCatalog.Default.Find(block.Type)!.IsComplete(block))
                {
                    // Save the page the block is in, such as StilPageJson.Serialize(page) to your database.
                }
            }
        }
        """;
}
